# Repo-level suppression (ADR-035).
#
# VAULT-PERSONAL is a PERSONAL-vault rule from the global ~/.config/keur/rules/
# layer. It is meant for personal notes, not for this public tool repo, where
# the owner's handle legitimately appears in the repo slug / installer / docs
# (authorship, not a leak — ADR-043). Scoping it off here is a scope correction,
# not a bypass. A finer per-term allow ("allow <term>" in the scan layer) is
# designed in ADR-046 and will replace this blunt rule-level suppression.
VAULT-PERSONAL

# VAULT-GDPR_UK is likewise a PERSONAL-vault rule from the global
# ~/.config/keur/rules/ layer (scans prose for GDPR/PII terms). On this public
# tool repo it only ever fires on binary assets (e.g. demos/*.gif, where image
# bytes coincidentally match a term) — a false positive, not a leak. Scoping it
# off here is a scope correction, same as VAULT-PERSONAL above (ADR-035/043).
VAULT-GDPR_UK

# VAULT-WORK is the work-vault rule from the global ~/.config/keur/rules/ layer.
# On this public tool repo its only hits are in config/disabled unit tests
# (src/keur/config) that use the rule id "VAULT-WORK" as test data to prove the
# scope-resolution logic — you cannot test resolving VAULT-WORK without naming
# it. The company name that used to be in the id was renamed out; what remains
# is a self-referential test, not a leak. Scope correction (ADR-035/043/046).
VAULT-WORK

# VAULT-GDPR_NL is the Dutch sibling of VAULT-GDPR_UK: a PERSONAL-vault rule from
# the global ~/.config/keur/rules/ layer that scans prose for GDPR/PII terms. On
# this public tool repo it hits ordinary English words in rule comments that are
# already public on main (seen when ADR-079 touched rules/secrets and
# rules/security). That is a false positive, not personal data; scoping it off is
# a scope correction, same as VAULT-GDPR_UK above (ADR-035/043). loods does the same.
VAULT-GDPR_NL

# SEC-BIDI-001 fires on docs/demo/*.gif — those are binary VHS recordings whose
# image bytes coincidentally contain the U+202x byte sequences. It is a false
# positive on a binary asset, not a Trojan-Source char in reviewable text. The
# rule stays active for real source; this suppresses the binary-asset hits only.
# Scoped by path (a bare rule id would silence it in EVERY file): the demo GIFs,
# and the rule's own file, which contains the characters as its test examples.
.gif:SEC-BIDI-001
rules/security/SEC-BIDI-001-trojan-source-control-chars.rule:SEC-BIDI-001

# VAULT-GENERIC is a PERSONAL-vault rule from the global ~/.config/keur/rules/
# layer. On docs/demo/pii-in-code.tape it fires on a DELIBERATE example PII value
# (jan.devries@acme.com) that the PII demo exists to show — demo fixture, not a
# leak. Scope correction, same as the other VAULT-* entries above (ADR-035/043).
VAULT-GENERIC
